{"timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:44:25.281Z","ordinal":399341,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a17b3e-a441-7d60-8734-c23b119da9e6","author":"/root/plan05_readonly_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/plan05_readonly_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — exact head `3a7386df8b3143faa1dc273ae50319003d0485e6`, base `16ee8ca3643824741b6da67936b41f262414fdf0`. Diff SHA-256: `092f7a7b19188a20d95f718a5fd48158dbe439713efed2ce3dd0908fa61c1e5e`.\n\nThere is a false-binding case in [tester.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/python-verifier-binding/cli/src/tester.ts:558): `from package import name` is treated as importing `package.name.py` whenever that file exists. Python first uses an existing package attribute with that name, so a test can import `package.name` from `package/__init__.py` while never importing the changed `package/name.py`. The verifier could then mark that deliverable `VERIFIED` without evidence that its module was imported. The new positive test at [verification_binding.test.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/python-verifier-binding/cli/test/verification_binding.test.ts:211) covers only the case where the attribute is absent.\n\nPlease treat ambiguous `from package import name` forms as unbound unless resolution can establish that Python loads the submodule, and add a regression with both `package/__init__.py` defining `name` and `package/name.py` present.\n\nThe other supported forms are conservative: relative and multiline imports remain unbound, consistent with the implementation’s stated “unknown layouts remain unbound” behavior. I did not run tests. "}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17b23-4ce0-7c11-90c6-74e78e580449","create_time":1793069065.2813816}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":19584}}
